Rejected experiment, kept for the record rather than as a 423 KiB copy of
metal/moe.metal that silently goes stale.

What it does: unpacks an IQ2_XXS grid entry from one uint64 in two 32-bit
halves instead of eight threadgroup byte loads, and derives the sign from
1 << j instead of a lookup table.

Why it is not in the tree:

  - It is not faster. The standalone bench measured no difference to -23%
    depending on the variant (see ds4-macos/V4.1-TUNING.md, "What did not
    work").
  - It changes the reduction order. The original adds terms j = 0..7 in
    order; this adds j and j+4 interleaved, so the row sums do not match the
    single-row kernel bit for bit. V4.1 pins its small-batch MoE to per-row
    mul_mv precisely to keep that agreement, so this would have to be
    justified against exactness even if it had been faster.

To try it again, apply to metal/moe.metal in the ds4 tree. Do not revive the
whole-file copy: a full duplicate reached through DS4_METAL_MOE_SOURCE also
carries every other kernel at the version it was copied at, which is how a
benchmark ends up measuring something nobody chose.

diff --git a/metal/moe.metal b/metal/moe.metal
--- a/metal/moe.metal
+++ b/metal/moe.metal
@@ -3211,15 +3211,28 @@
 
             float sg = 0;
             float su = 0;
+            /* A grid entry is eight packed int8 values in one uint64.  Read it
+             * once and unpack from two 32-bit halves instead of taking eight
+             * threadgroup byte loads; the sign mask is 1 << j, so that table
+             * lookup goes as well. */
             for (short l = 0; l < 4; ++l) {
-                const threadgroup uint8_t * gridg = (const threadgroup uint8_t *)(svalues + aux8g[l]);
-                const threadgroup uint8_t * gridu = (const threadgroup uint8_t *)(svalues + aux8u[l]);
+                const uint64_t gg = svalues[aux8g[l]];
+                const uint64_t gu = svalues[aux8u[l]];
+                const uint gg_lo = (uint)gg, gg_hi = (uint)(gg >> 32);
+                const uint gu_lo = (uint)gu, gu_hi = (uint)(gu >> 32);
                 const uint8_t signg = ssigns[(aux32g >> 7*l) & 127];
                 const uint8_t signu = ssigns[(aux32u >> 7*l) & 127];
-                for (short j = 0; j < 8; ++j) {
-                    const float v = yl[8*l + j];
-                    sg += v * gridg[j] * (signg & ds4_metal_kmask_iq2xs[j] ? -1.f : 1.f);
-                    su += v * gridu[j] * (signu & ds4_metal_kmask_iq2xs[j] ? -1.f : 1.f);
+                for (short j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {
+                    const float v0 = yl[8*l + j];
+                    const float v1 = yl[8*l + j + 4];
+                    const float g0 = v0 * (float)((gg_lo >> (8*j)) & 0xffu);
+                    const float g1 = v1 * (float)((gg_hi >> (8*j)) & 0xffu);
+                    const float u0 = v0 * (float)((gu_lo >> (8*j)) & 0xffu);
+                    const float u1 = v1 * (float)((gu_hi >> (8*j)) & 0xffu);
+                    sg += ((signg >> j)       & 1) ? -g0 : g0;
+                    sg += ((signg >> (j + 4)) & 1) ? -g1 : g1;
+                    su += ((signu >> j)       & 1) ? -u0 : u0;
+                    su += ((signu >> (j + 4)) & 1) ? -u1 : u1;
                 }
             }
             sumg[row] += dg * sg;
